# Everything text is LF in the working tree, on every host.
#
# Without this line the per-type rules below are the only pins, and this host's
# system-wide core.autocrlf=true checks out everything else as CRLF. Windows git
# then normalises on read and calls the tree clean, while a git inside WSL - which
# has no autocrlf - reports the same 40+ files as modified. Neither is wrong; they
# are reading the same blobs through different filters. Pinning eol=lf makes the
# working tree itself the same bytes on both sides, so `git status` agrees.
#
# text=auto still lets git detect and skip binary content, and every rule below
# this one keeps overriding it: later rules win.
* text=auto eol=lf
# ...except Windows batch files, which cmd.exe parses line-by-line.
*.bat text eol=crlf
# Shell scripts MUST stay LF.
#
# device-files/arca-app and the other device scripts are scp'd
# byte-for-byte onto the device and run by BusyBox sh. A CRLF line ending turns
# the shebang into `#!/bin/sh\r`, which BusyBox rejects with a misleading "no
# such file or directory" - the device then boots to a black screen with no
# launcher and no obvious cause.
#
# This host has core.autocrlf=true set system-wide, so without this file the
# next clone on Windows would silently break the device.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Python helpers are also run on the device / under WSL
*.py text eol=lf
# Device-side config and manifest formats
*.conf text eol=lf
*.app  text eol=lf
# device-files/ is installed onto the device VERBATIM by two consumers
# (provision.sh over SSH, rootfs/commission-offline.sh onto
# a mounted card), and its init scripts have no .sh extension because
# /etc/init.d/audio-enable is the name the rc5.d link points at. So the rule above
# does not cover them, and a CRLF here breaks the boot exactly as described at the
# top of this file.
device-files/** text eol=lf
# Binary assets - never touch
*.ppm binary
*.raw binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.img binary
*.zip binary
*.dat binary
*.wav binary
*.bin binary
# Teardown photos (cited throughout HARDWARE.md) are the only images the repo
# carries; they are stored in Git LFS so a clone stays small. This must come
# AFTER the `*.jpg binary` line above - later rules win, and `binary` would
# otherwise clobber the diff/merge attributes LFS needs.
# Requires `git lfs install` before cloning, or the working copy gets pointer
# text files instead of JPEGs.
HardwarePhotos/** filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The operator's BeagleBone Black photos, same reasoning.
beaglebone/image/** filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Documentation screenshots, same reasoning as above.
Screenshots/** filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The music beds are large binaries, so they are LFS objects.  ⚠️ The effect WAVs
# under native_apps/sounds/ are DELIBERATELY not here: they were checked and left at
# `filter: unspecified` because each is a few KB and regenerable, and adding them
# would make every clone need git-lfs to get a playable tree.  Do not "fix" this.
native_apps/music/** filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
